.RE
.sp


These codes are replaced with the values for the file, and the resulting strings can be compared with the following operations:


.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
s1 equals s2: true if s1 and s2 are equal\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
s1 contains s2: true if s1 contains s2,
i\&.e\&.
s2 is a substring of s1\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
s matches re: true if s matches the regular expression re\&.
.RE
.sp

True expressions are replaced by 1, false by 0\&. True values are represented by 1, true, on and yes, false values by 0, false, off and no\&. Boolean operations are not, and, or (in this order of precedence) and can be grouped by parentheses\&.
.sp

Some filter rules are predefined and can serve as examples for your own expressions:


.PP
All
.RS 4

When the file list is filtered \- this is shown by "[filtered]" in the window title \- and all files shall be displayed again, the filtering can be reverted using this filter\&. It uses an empty expression, but a true value would have the same effect\&.
.RE
.PP
Filename Tag Mismatch
.RS 4

\fB not (%{filepath} contains "%{artist} \- %{album}/%{track} %{title}") \fR
.sp

Tests if the file path conforms with the file name format\&. This rule is automatically adapted if the file name format changes\&.
.RE
.PP
No Tag 1
.RS 4

\fB %{tag1} equals "" \fR
.sp

Displays only files which do not have a tag 1\&.
.RE
.PP
No Tag 2
.RS 4

\fB %{tag2} equals "" \fR
.sp

Displays only files which do not have a tag 2\&.
.RE
.PP
ID3v2\&.3\&.0 Tag
.RS 4

\fB %{tag2} equals "ID3v2\&.3\&.0" \fR
.sp

Displays only files which have an ID3v2\&.3\&.0 tag\&.
.RE
.PP
ID3v2\&.4\&.0 Tag
.RS 4

\fB %{tag2} equals "ID3v2\&.4\&.0" \fR
.sp

Displays only files which have an ID3v2\&.4\&.0 tag\&.
.RE
.PP
Tag 1 != Tag 2
.RS 4

\fB not (%1{title} equals %2{title} and %1{album} equals %2{album} and %1{artist} equals %2{artist} and %1{comment} equals %2{comment} and %1{year} equals %2{year} and %1{track} equals %2{track} and %1{genre} equals %2{genre}) \fR
.sp

Displays files with differences between tag 1 and tag2\&.
.RE
.PP
Tag 1 == Tag 2
.RS 4

\fB %1{title} equals %2{title} and %1{album} equals %2{album} and %1{artist} equals %2{artist} and %1{comment} equals %2{comment} and %1{year} equals %2{year} and %1{track} equals %2{track} and %1{genre} equals %2{genre} \fR
.sp

Displays files with identical tag 1 and tag 2\&.
.RE
.PP
Incomplete
.RS 4

\fB %{title} equals "" or %{artist} equals "" or %{album} equals "" or %{year} equals "" or %{tracknumber} equals "" or %{genre} equals "" \fR
.sp

Displays files with empty values in the standard tags (title, artist, album, date, track number, genre)\&.
.RE
.PP
No Picture
.RS 4

\fB %{picture} equals "" \fR
.sp

Displays only files which do not have a picture\&.
.RE
.PP
Marked
.RS 4

\fB not (%{marked} equals "") \fR
.sp

Displays only files which are marked because they violate the ID3 standard, are truncated or the picture is too large\&.
.RE
.PP
Custom Filter
.RS 4

To add your own filter, select this entry\&. For instance, if you want to have a filter for artists starting with "The", replace "Custom Filter" with the name "The Bands" and press
Enter\&. Then insert the following expression into the line edit:
.sp

\fB %{artist} matches "The\&.*" \fR
.sp

Then click
Save Settings\&. Click
Apply
to filter the files\&. All files processed are displayed in the text view, with a "+" for those who match the filter and a "\-" for the others\&. When finished, only the files with an artist starting with "The" are displayed, and the window title is marked with "[filtered]"\&.
.RE
.sp

.RE
.PP
Tools → Convert ID3v2\&.3 to ID3v2\&.4
.RS 4


If there are any ID3v2\&.3 tags in the selected files, they will be
converted to ID3v2\&.4
tags\&. Frames which are not supported by TagLib will be discarded\&. Only files without unsaved changes will be converted\&.
.RE
.PP
Tools → Convert ID3v2\&.4 to ID3v2\&.3
.RS 4


If there are any ID3v2\&.4 tags in the selected files, they will be
converted to ID3v2\&.3
tags\&. Only files without unsaved changes will be converted\&.
.RE
.PP
Tools → Play
.RS 4


This opens a simple toolbar to play audio files\&. It contains buttons for the basic operations (Play/Pause,
Stop playback,
Previous Track,
Next Track,
Close), sliders for position and volume and a display of the current position\&. If multiple files are selected, the selected tracks are played, else all files will be played\&.
.sp
The time displayed can be toggled between elapsed and remaining time by clicking on the display\&.
.RE




.SS "The Settings Menu"

.PP


.PP
Settings → Show Toolbar
.RS 4


Toggles displaying of the toolbar\&.
.RE
.PP
Settings → Show Statusbar
.RS 4


Toggles displaying of the statusbar, which displays longer actions such as opening or saving a folder\&.
.RE
.PP
Settings → Show Picture
.RS 4


Toggles displaying of the album cover art preview picture\&.
.RE
.PP
Settings → Auto Hide Tags
.RS 4


Empty tags are automatically hidden if this option is active\&. The
File,
Tag 1
and
Tag 2
sections can be manually collapsed and expanded by clicking on the corresponding
\-/+
buttons\&.
.RE
.PP
Settings → Configure Shortcut keys\&.\&.\&.
.RS 4


Opens a dialog to assign keyboard shortcuts for most of the program functions\&. There are even functions without corresponding menu or button available,
e\&.g\&.
next file, previous file, select all\&.
.sp



.RE
.PP
Settings → Configure Kid3\&.\&.\&.
.RS 4


Opens the
configuration dialog, which consists of pages for tags, files, user actions, and network settings\&.
.sp

Tag specific options can be found on the
Tags
page, which is itself separated into four tabs for
Tag 1,
Tag 2,
Tag 3, and
All Tags\&.
.sp

If
Mark truncated fields
is checked, truncated ID3v1\&.1 fields will be marked red\&. The text fields of ID3v1\&.1 tags can only have 30 characters, the comment only 28 characters\&. Also the genre and track numbers are restricted, so that fields can be truncated when imported or transferred from ID3v2\&. Truncated fields and the file will be marked red, and the mark will be removed after the field has been edited\&.
.sp

With
Text encoding
for
ID3v1
it is possible to set the character set used in ID3v1 tags\&. This encoding is supposed to be ISO\-8859\-1, so it is recommended to keep this default value\&. However, there are tags around with different encoding, so it can be set here and the ID3v1 tags can then be copied to ID3v2 which supports Unicode\&.
.sp

The check box
Use track/total number of tracks format
controls whether the track number field of ID3v2 tags contains simply the track number or additionally the total number of tracks in the folder\&.
.sp

When
Genre as text instead of numeric string
is checked, all ID3v2 genres will be stored as a text string even if there is a corresponding code for ID3v1 genres\&. If this option is not set, genres for which an ID3v1 code exists are stored as the number of the genre code (in parentheses for ID3v2\&.3)\&. Thus the genre Metal is stored as "Metal" or "(9)" depending on this option\&. Genres which are not in the list of ID3v1 genres are always stored as a text string\&. The purpose of this option is improved compatibility with devices which do not correctly interpret genre codes\&.
.sp

When
WAV files with lowercase id3 chunk
is checked, the RIFF chunk used to store ID3v2 tags in WAV files will be named "id3 " instead of "ID3 "\&. By default,
Kid3
and other applications using TagLib accept both the lowercase and the uppercase variant when reading WAV files, but they use "ID3 " when writing ID3v2 tags to WAV files\&. As there exist other applications which only accept "id3 " (e\&.g\&.
JRiver Media Center and foobar2000), this option can be used to create tags which can be read by such applications\&.
.sp

When
Mark standard violations
is checked, ID3v2 fields which violate the standard will be marked red\&. Details about the violation are shown in a tooltip:


.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must be unique
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
New line is forbidden
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Carriage return is forbidden
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Owner must be non\-empty
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must be numeric
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must be numeric or number/total
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Format is DDMM
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Format is HHMM
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Format is YYYY
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must begin with a year and a space character
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must be ISO 8601 date/time
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must be musical key, 3 characters, A\-G, b, #, m, o
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must have ISO 639\-2 language code, 3 lowercase characters
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must be ISRC code, 12 characters
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Must be list of strings separated by \*(Aq|\*(Aq
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Has excess white space
.RE
.sp

The ID3 standard documents are available online:


.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\m[blue]\fBID3 tag version 2\&.3\&.0\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[11]\d\s+2
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\m[blue]\fBID3 tag version 2\&.4\&.0 \- Main Structure\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[12]\d\s+2
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\m[blue]\fBID3 tag version 2\&.4\&.0 \- Native Frames\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[5]\d\s+2
.RE
.sp

Text encoding
defines the default encoding used for ID3v2 frames and can be set to
ISO\-8859\-1,
UTF16, or
UTF8\&.
UTF8
is not valid for ID3v2\&.3\&.0 frames; if it is set,
UTF16
will be used instead\&. For ID3v2\&.4\&.0 frames, all three encodings are possible\&.
.sp

Version used for new tags
determines whether new ID3v2 tags are created as version 2\&.3\&.0 or 2\&.4\&.0\&.
.sp

Track number digits
is the number of digits in Track Number fields\&. Leading zeros are used to pad\&. For instance, with a value of 2 the track number 5 is set as "05"\&.
.sp

The combo box
Comment field name
is only relevant for Ogg/Vorbis and FLAC files and sets the name of the field used for comments\&. Different applications seem to use different names, "COMMENT" for instance is used by
XMMS, whereas
Amarok
uses "DESCRIPTION"\&.
.sp

The format of pictures in Ogg/Vorbis files is determined by
Picture field name, which can be "METADATA_BLOCK_PICTURE" or "COVERART"\&. The first is the official standard and uses the same format as pictures in FLAC tags\&. "COVERART" is an earlier unofficial way to include pictures in Vorbis comments\&. It can be used for compatibility with legacy players\&.
.sp

The mode used when saving
Matroska
files can be selected with
Write mode\&. It controls the trade\-off between file size and write speed when saving tags, attachments and chapters to the file\&. For very large files and/or slow (network) filesystems, using
Avoid insert
will reduce write time significantly\&. There are three possible modes:


.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Compact: Write tags, attachments and chapters as compact as possible\&. This is the default mode\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Do not shrink: Do not shrink blocks when writing, add padding when content gets smaller\&. Allow inserts when content gets larger\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Avoid insert: Like
Do not shrink
but also avoid inserts for blocks which are not at the end of the file: Replace a growing non\-last block with padding of the old size and append a new block at the end of the file\&.
.RE
.sp

If the
Mark if larger than (bytes)
check box is activated, files containing embedded album cover art exceeding the given size in bytes are marked red\&. This can be used to find files containing oversized pictures which are not accepted by some applications and players\&. The default value is 131072 bytes (128 KB)\&.
.sp

Custom Genres
can be used to define genres which are not available in the standard genre list,
e\&.g\&.
"Gothic Metal"\&. Such custom genres will appear in the
Genre
combo box of
Tag 2\&. For ID3v1\&.1 tags, only the predefined genres can be used\&.
.sp

The list of custom genres can also be used to reduce the number of genres available in the
Genre
combo box to those typically used\&. If your collection mostly contains music in the genres Metal, Gothic Metal, Ancient and Hard Rock, you can enter those genres and mark
Show only custom genres\&. The
Tag 2
Genre
combo box will then only contain those four genres and you will not have to search through the complete genres list for them\&. In this example, only Metal and Hard Rock will be listed in the tag 1 genres list, because those two custom genres entries are standard genres\&. If
Show only custom genres
is not active, the custom genres can be found at the end of the genres list\&.
.sp

In
Custom Frames, up to eight custom frame names can be defined, which can then be used like the unified frames, for example as quick access frames\&.
.sp

Quick Access Frames
defines which frame types are always shown in the
Tag 2
section\&. Such frames can then be added without first using the
Add
button\&. The order of these quick access frames can be changed by dragging and dropping items\&.
.sp

The combo box
Track number field name
is only relevant for RIFF INFO and sets the name of the field used for track numbers\&. Track numbers are not specified in the original RIFF standard, there are applications which use "ITRK", others use "IPRT"\&.
.sp

Tag Format
contains options for the format of the tags\&. When
Automatically apply format
is checked, the format configuration is automatically used while editing text in the line edits\&.
Validation
enables validators in the controls with track/total and date/time values\&. The
Case conversion
can be set to
No changes,
All lowercase,
All uppercase,
First letter uppercase
or
All first letters uppercase\&. To use locale\-aware conversion between lowercase and uppercase characters, a locale can be selected in the combobox below\&. The string replacement list can be set to arbitrary string mappings\&. To add a new mapping, select the
From
cell of a row and insert the text to replace, then go to the
To
column and enter the replacement text\&. When the text to replace starts and ends with a slash ("/"), a regular expression is used\&. For regular expressions containing capturing groups, occurrences of \e1, \e2, \&.\&.\&. in
To
are replaced with the string captured by the corresponding capturing group\&. To remove a mapping set the
From
cell to an empty value (e\&.g\&.
by first typing space and then backspace)\&. Inserting and deleting rows is also possible using a context menu which appears when the
right
mouse button is clicked\&. Replacement is only active, if the
String replacement
check box is checked\&.
.sp


The table in
Rating
contains the mapping of star ratings to the effective values stored in the tag\&. The frames with rating information are listed in the Rating row of the frame list\&. For these frames, the rating can be set by giving a number of stars out of five stars\&. Different tag formats and different applications use different values to map the star rating to the value stored in the tag\&. In order to display the correct number of stars,
Kid3
will look up a map in this table\&. The key to look up the mapping is the frame name, for example "RATING" as used for Vorbis comments or "IRTD" for RIFF INFO\&. For ID3v2 tags, a combined key is used consisting of the frame ID "POPM" of the Popularimeter frame and its "Email" field, separated by a dot\&. Therefore, different keys for ID3v2 exist,
e\&.g\&.
"POPM\&.Windows Media Player 9 Series" for the mapping used by Windows Media Player and Explorer, and simply "POPM" for POPM frames with an empty "Email" field\&. As multiple entries for "POPM" can exist, their order is important\&. When
Kid3
adds a new Popularimeter frame, it will use the first "POPM" entry to determine the value to be written into the "Email" field\&. This value will then specify the mapping to be used for star ratings\&. The first entry is also used if no key was found, it is therefore the default entry\&.
.sp

Besides the
Name
column containing the keys, the table has columns
1
to
5
for the values to be stored when the corresponding number of stars is given\&. The other way round, the values determine the number of stars which are displayed for the value stored in the frame\&. For instance, the row in the table below contains the values 1, 64, 128, 196, 255\&. The thresholds for the number of stars to be displayed lay between these values and are compatible with what the
Windows\(rg
Explorer uses\&.

.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.B Table\ \&1.\ \&Entry in Rating Table
.TS
allbox tab(:);
lB lB lB lB lB lB.
T{
Name
T}:T{
1
T}:T{
2
T}:T{
3
T}:T{
4
T}:T{
5
T}
.T&
l l l l l l
l l l l l l.
T{
POPM
T}:T{
1
T}:T{
64
T}:T{
128
T}:T{
196
T}:T{
255
T}
T{
Range
T}:T{
1\-31
T}:T{
32\-95
T}:T{
96\-159
T}:T{
160\-223
T}:T{
224\-255
T}
.TE
.sp 1


On the page
Files
the check box
Load last\-opened files
can be marked so that
Kid3
will open and select the last selected file when it is started the next time\&.
Preserve file timestamp
can be checked to preserve the file modification time stamp\&.
Filename for cover
sets the name which is suggested when an embedded image is exported to a file\&. With
Text encoding (Export, Playlist)
the encoding used when writing files can be set\&. The default
System
can be changed for example if playlists have to be used on a different device\&.
.sp

If
Mark changes
is active, changed fields are marked with a light gray label background\&.
.sp

The section
File List
determines which files are displayed in the file list\&. A
Filter
can be used to restrict the items in this list to files with supported extensions\&. To explicitly specify which folders to display in the file list or exclude certain folders, the options
Include folders
and
Exclude folders
can be used\&. They can contain wildcard expressions, for instance
*/Music/*
to include only the
Music
folder, or
*/iTunes/*
to exclude the iTunes folder from the file list\&. If multiple such expressions have to be used, they can be separated by spaces or semicolons\&.
.sp

The buttons
Filename from tag
and
Tag from filename
in section
Format
open dialogs to edit the formats which are available in the
Format
combo boxes (with arrows up and down), which can be found in the
File
section of the main window\&.
.sp

The
Playlist
button can be used to edit the file name formats available in the
Create Playlist
dialog\&.
.sp

Filename Format
contains options for the format of the filenames\&. The same options as in
Tag Format
are available\&.
.sp

Additionally, the
Maximum length
allowed for file names can be set\&. Most modern file systems have a limit of 255 characters, but if you want to burn the files to CD, you should set the limit to 64\&. If
Use for playlist and folder names
is checked, the file name format is also used when creating playlists and renaming folders\&.
.sp

The
User Actions
page contains a table with the commands which are available in the context menu of the file list\&. For critical operations such as deleting files, it is advisable to mark
Confirm
to pop up a confirmation dialog before executing the command\&.
Output
can be marked to see the output written by console commands (standard output and standard error)\&.
Name
is the name displayed in the context menu\&.
Command
is the command line to be executed\&. Arguments can be passed using the following codes:



.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%F %{files} File paths (a list if multiple files selected)
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%f %{file} File path to single file
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%uF %{urls}
URLs (a list if multiple files selected)
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%uf %{url}
URL
to single file
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%d %{directory} Folder
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%s %{title} Title (Song)
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%a %{artist} Artist
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%l %{album} Album
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%c %{comment} Comment
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%y %{year} Year
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%t %{track} Track (e\&.g\&.
01)
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%t %{track\&.n} Track with field width n (e\&.g\&.
001 for %{track\&.3})
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%T %{tracknumber} Track (without leading zeros,
e\&.g\&.
1)
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%g %{genre} Genre
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%b %{browser} Command to start the web browser
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
%q %{qmlpath} Base folder of provided QML files
.RE
.sp


The special code
\fB@separator\fR
can be set as a command to insert a separator into the user actions context menu\&. Menu items can be put into a submenu by enclosing them with
\fB@beginmenu\fR
and
\fB@endmenu\fR
commands\&. The name of the submenu is determined by the
Name
column of the
\fB@beginmenu\fR
command\&.
.sp


To execute QML scripts,
\fB@qml\fR
is used as a command name\&. The path to the QML script is passed as a parameter\&. The provided scripts can be found in the folder
%{qmlpath}/script/
(on
Linux\(rg
typically
/usr/share/kid3/qml/script/, on Windows
qml/script/
inside the installation folder, and on
macOS\(rg
in the app folder
kid3\&.app/Contents/Resources/qml/script/)\&. Custom scripts can be stored in any folder\&. If the QML code uses
GUI
components,
\fB@qmlview\fR
shall be used instead of
\fB@qml\fR\&. Additional parameters are passed to the QML script where they will be available via the
\fBgetArguments()\fR
function\&. An overview of some functions and properties which are available in QML can be found in the appendix
QML Interface\&.
.sp


The command which will be inserted with %{browser} can be defined in the
Web browser
line edit above\&. Commands starting with %{browser} can be used to fetch information about the audio files from the web, for instance
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
\fB%{browser} http://lyricwiki\&.org/%u{artist}:%u{title}\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
will query the lyrics for the current song in
\m[blue]\fBLyricWiki\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[13]\d\s+2\&. The "u" in %u{artist} and %u{title} is used to
URL\-encode the artist %{artist} and song %{title} information\&. It is easy to define your own queries in the same way,
e\&.g\&.
an image search with
\m[blue]\fBGoogle\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[14]\d\s+2:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
\fB%{browser} http://images\&.google\&.com/images?q=%u{artist}%20%u{album}\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

To add album cover art to tag 2, you can search for images with Google or Amazon using the commands described above\&. The picture can be added to the tag with drag and drop\&. You can also add an image with
Add, then select the Picture frame and import an image file or paste from the clipboard\&. Picture frames are supported for ID3v2, MP4, FLAC, Ogg and ASF tags\&.
.sp

To add and delete entries in the table, a context menu can be used\&.
.sp

The
Network
page contains only a field to insert the proxy address and optionally the port, separated by a colon\&. The proxy will be used when importing from an Internet server when the check box is checked\&.
.sp

In the
Plugins
page, available plugins can be enabled or disabled\&. The plugins are separated into two sections\&. The
Metadata Plugins & Priority
list contains plugins which support audio file formats\&. The order of the plugins is important because they are tried from top to bottom\&. Some formats are supported by multiple plugins, so files will be opened with the first plugin supporting them\&. The
TaglibMetadata
supports most formats, if it is at the top of the list, it will open most of the files\&. If you want to use a different plugin for a file format, make sure that it is listed before the
TaglibMetadata
plugin\&. Details about the metadata plugin and why you may want to use them instead of TagLib are listed below\&.


.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Id3libMetadata: Uses
\m[blue]\fBid3lib\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[15]\d\s+2
for ID3v1\&.1 and ID3v2\&.3 tags in MP3, MP2, AAC files\&. Supports a few more frame types than TagLib\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
OggFlacMetadata: Uses
\m[blue]\fBlibogg\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[16]\d\s+2,
\m[blue]\fBlibvorbis, libvorbisfile\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[17]\d\s+2
for Ogg files, and additionally
\m[blue]\fBlibFLAC++ and libFLAC\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[18]\d\s+2
for FLAC files\&. These are the official libraries for these formats\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
TaglibMetadata: Uses
\m[blue]\fBTagLib\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[19]\d\s+2
which supports a lot of audio file formats\&. It can be used for all audio files supported by
Kid3\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Mp4v2Metadata:
\m[blue]\fBmp4v2\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[20]\d\s+2
was originally used by
Kid3
to support M4A files\&. Can be used in case of problems with the M4A support of TagLib\&.
.RE
.sp

The
Available Plugins
section lists the remaining plugins\&. Their order is not important, but they can be enabled or disabled using the check boxes\&.


.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
AmazonImport: Used for the
Import from Amazon\&.\&.\&.
function\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
DiscogsImport: Used for the
Import from Discogs\&.\&.\&.
function\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
FreedbImport: Used for the
Import from gnudb\&.org\&.\&.\&.
function\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
MusicBrainzImport: Used for the
Import from MusicBrainz Release\&.\&.\&.
function\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
AcoustidImport: Used for the
Import from MusicBrainz Fingerprint\&.\&.\&.
function, which depends on the
\m[blue]\fBChromaprint\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[21]\d\s+2
and
\m[blue]\fBlibav\fR\m[]\&\s-2\u[22]\d\s+2
libraries\&.
.RE
.sp

Plugins which are disabled will not be loaded\&. This can be used to optimize resource usage and startup time\&. The settings on this page take only effect after a restart of
Kid3\&.

.RE





.SS "The Help Menu"

.PP


.PP
Help → Kid3 Handbook
.RS 4


Opens this handbook\&.
.RE
.PP
Help → About Kid3
.RS 4


Displays a short information about Kid3\&.
.RE








.SH "KID3\-CLI"

.SS "Commands"

.PP
\fBkid3\-cli\fR
offers a command\-line\-interface for
Kid3\&. If a folder path is used, the folder is opened\&. If one or more file paths are given, the common folder is opened and the files are selected\&. Subsequent commands will then work on these files\&. Commands are specified using
\fB\-c\fR
options\&. If multiple commands are passed, they are executed in the given order\&. If files are modified by the commands, they will be saved at the end\&. If no command options are passed,
\fBkid3\-cli\fR
starts in interactive mode\&. Commands can be entered and will operate on the current selection\&. The following sections list all available commands\&.

.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBHelp\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBhelp\fR\ 'u

\fBhelp\fR
 [\fICOMMAND\-NAME\fR]

.PP
Displays help about the parameters of
\fICOMMAND\-NAME\fR
or about all commands if no command name is given\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBTimeout\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBtimeout\fR\ 'u

\fBtimeout\fR
 [
 | default
 | off
 | \fITIME\fR
]

.PP
Overwrite the default command timeout\&. The CLI commands abort after a command specific timeout is expired\&. This timeout is 10 seconds for
\fBls\fR
and
\fBalbumart\fR, 60 seconds for
\fBautoimport\fR
and
\fBfilter\fR, and 3 seconds for all other commands\&. If a huge number of files has to be processed, these timeouts may be too restrictive, thus the timeout for all commands can be set to
\fITIME\fR
ms, switched off altogether or be left at the default values\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBQuit application\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBexit\fR\ 'u

\fBexit\fR
 [force]

.PP
Exit application\&. If there are modified unsaved files, the
\fIforce\fR
parameter is required\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBChange folder\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBcd\fR\ 'u

\fBcd\fR
 [\fIFOLDER\fR]

.PP
If no
\fIFOLDER\fR
is given, change to the home folder\&. If a folder is given, change into the folder\&. If one or more file paths are given, change to their common folder and select the files\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBPrint the filename of the current folder\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBpwd\fR\ 'u

\fBpwd\fR

.PP
Print the filename of the current working folder\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBFolder list\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBls\fR\ 'u

\fBls\fR

.PP
List the contents of the current folder\&. This corresponds to the file list in the
Kid3
GUI\&. Five characters before the file names show the state of the file\&.


.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
>
File is selected\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
*
File is modified\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
1
File has a tag 1, otherwise \*(Aq\-\*(Aq is displayed\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
2
File has a tag 2, otherwise \*(Aq\-\*(Aq is displayed\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
3
File has a tag 3, otherwise \*(Aq\-\*(Aq is displayed\&.
.RE

.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
kid3\-cli> \fBls\fR
  1\-\- 01 Intro\&.mp3
> 12\- 02 We Only Got This One\&.mp3
 *1\-\- 03 Outro\&.mp3
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

.PP
In this example, all files have a tag 1, the second file also has a tag 2 and it is selected\&. The third file is modified\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBSave the changed files\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBsave\fR\ 'u

\fBsave\fR

.PP

.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBSelect file\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBselect\fR\ 'u

\fBselect\fR
 [
 | all
 | none
 | first
 | previous
 | next
 | \fIFILE\fR...
]

.PP
To select all files, enter
\fBselect all\fR, to deselect all files, enter
\fBselect none\fR\&. To traverse the files in the current folder start with
\fBselect first\fR, then go forward using
\fBselect next\fR
or backward using
\fBselect previous\fR\&. Specific files can be added to the current selection by giving their file names\&. Wildcards are possible, so
\fBselect *\&.mp3\fR
will select all MP3 files in the current folder\&.


.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
kid3\-cli> \fBselect first\fR
kid3\-cli> \fBls\fR
> 1\-\- 01 Intro\&.mp3
  12\- 02 We Only Got This One\&.mp3
 *1\-\- 03 Outro\&.mp3
kid3\-cli> \fBselect next\fR
kid3\-cli> \fBls\fR
  1\-\- 01 Intro\&.mp3
> 12\- 02 We Only Got This One\&.mp3
 *1\-\- 03 Outro\&.mp3
kid3\-cli> \fBselect *\&.mp3\fR
kid3\-cli> \fBls\fR
> 1\-\- 01 Intro\&.mp3
> 12\- 02 We Only Got This One\&.mp3
>*1\-\- 03 Outro\&.mp3
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBSelect tag\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBtag\fR\ 'u

\fBtag\fR
 [\fITAG\-NUMBERS\fR]

.PP
Many commands have an optional
\fITAG\-NUMBERS\fR
parameter, which specifies whether the command operates on tag 1, 2, or 3\&. If this parameter is omitted, the default tag numbers are used, which can be set by this command\&. At startup, it is set to
12
which means that information is read from tag 2 if available, else from tag 1; modifications are done on tag 2\&. The
\fB\fITAG\-NUMBERS\fR\fR
can be set to
\fB1\fR,
\fB2\fR, or
\fB3\fR
to operate only on the corresponding tag\&. If the parameter is omitted, the current setting is displayed\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBGet tag frame\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBget\fR\ 'u

\fBget\fR
 [
 | all
 | \fIFRAME\-NAME\fR
]
 [\fITAG\-NUMBERS\fR]

.PP
This command can be used to read the value of a specific tag frame or get information about all tag frames (if the argument is omitted or
\fBall\fR
is used)\&. Modified frames are marked with a \*(Aq*\*(Aq\&.


.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
kid3\-cli> \fBget\fR
File: MPEG 1 Layer 3 192 kbps 44100 Hz Joint Stereo
  Name: 01 Intro\&.mp3
Tag 1: ID3v1\&.1
  Title         Intro
  Artist        One Hit Wonder
  Album         Let\*(Aqs Tag
  Date          2013
  Track Number  1
  Genre         Pop
kid3\-cli> \fBget title\fR
Intro
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.PP
To save the contents of a picture frame to a file, use
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
\fBget picture:\*(Aq/path/to/folder\&.jpg\*(Aq\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

.PP
To save synchronized lyrics to an LRC file, use
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
\fBget SYLT:\*(Aq/path/to/lyrics\&.lrc\*(Aq\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

.PP
It is possible to get only a specific field from a frame, for example
\fBget POPM\&.Email\fR
for the Email field of a Popularimeter frame\&. If a file has multiple frames of the same kind, the different frames can be indexed with brackets, for example the first performer from a Vorbis comment can be retrieved using
\fBget performer[0]\fR, the second using
\fBget performer[1]\fR\&.
.PP
The pseudo field name "selected" can be used to check if a frame is selected, for example
\fBget artist\&.selected\fR
will return
1
if the artist frame is selected, else
0\&.
.PP
The pseudo frame name "ratingstars" can be used to get the value of the "rating" frame as the format specific value corresponding to the number of stars (0 to 5)\&. When using "rating", the internal value is returned\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBSet tag frame\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBset\fR\ 'u

\fBset\fR
 {\fIFRAME\-NAME\fR}
 {\fIFRAME\-VALUE\fR}
 [\fITAG\-NUMBERS\fR]

.PP
This command sets the value of a specific tag frame\&. If
\fIFRAME\-VALUE\fR
is empty, the frame is deleted\&.

.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
kid3\-cli> \fBset remixer \*(AqO\&.H\&. Wonder\*(Aq\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.PP
To set the contents of a picture frame from a file, use
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
\fBset picture:\*(Aq/path/to/folder\&.jpg\*(Aq \*(AqPicture Description\*(Aq\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

.PP
To set synchronized lyrics from an LRC file, use
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
\fBset SYLT:\*(Aq/path/to/lyrics\&.lrc\*(Aq \*(AqLyrics Description\*(Aq\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

.PP
To set a specific field of a frame, the field name can be given after a dot,
e\&.g\&.
to set the Counter field of a Popularimeter frame, use
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
\fBset POPM\&.Counter 5\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

.PP
An application for field specifications is the case where you want a custom TXXX frame with "rating" description instead of a standard Popularimeter frame (this seems to be used by some plugins)\&. You can create such a TXXX rating frame with
\fBkid3\-cli\fR, however, you have to first create a TXXX frame with description "rating" and then set the value of this frame to the rating value\&.
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
kid3\-cli> \fBset rating ""\fR
kid3\-cli> \fBset TXXX\&.Description rating\fR
kid3\-cli> \fBset rating 5\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
The first command will delete an existing POPM frame, because if such a frame exists,
\fBset rating 5\fR
would set the POPM frame and not the TXXX frame\&. Another possibility would be to use
\fBset TXXX\&.Text 5\fR, but this would only work if there is no other TXXX frame present\&.
.PP
To set multiple frames of the same kind, an index can be given in brackets,
e\&.g\&.
to set multiple performers in a Vorbis comment, use
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
kid3\-cli> \fBset performer[0] \*(AqLiza don Getti (soprano)\*(Aq\fR
kid3\-cli> \fBset performer[1] \*(AqJoe Barr (piano)\*(Aq\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

.PP
To select certain frames before a copy, paste or remove action, the pseudo field name "selected" can be used\&. Normally, all frames are selected, to deselect all, use
\fBset \*(Aq*\&.selected\*(Aq 0\fR, then for example
\fBset artist\&.selected 1\fR
to select the artist frame\&.
.PP
The pseudo frame name "ratingstars" can be used to set the value of the "rating" frame to the format specific value corresponding to the number of stars (0 to 5)\&. The frame name "rating" can be used to set the internal value\&.
.PP
Setting "ratingstars" on multiple files having different tag formats will not work because the frame with the value mapped from the star count is created for the first file and then used for all files\&. So instead of
\fBkid3\-cli \-c "set ratingstars 2" *\fR
you should rather use
\fBfor f in *; do kid3\-cli \-c "set ratingstars 2" "$f"; done\fR\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBRevert\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBrevert\fR\ 'u

\fBrevert\fR

.PP
Revert all modifications in the selected files (or all files if no files are selected)\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBImport from file\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBimport\fR\ 'u

\fBimport\fR
 {\fIFILE\fR}
 {\fIFORMAT\-NAME\fR}
 [\fITAG\-NUMBERS\fR]

.PP
Tags are imported from the file
\fIFILE\fR
in the format with the name
\fIFORMAT\-NAME\fR
(e\&.g\&.
\fB"CSV unquoted"\fR, see
Import)\&.
.PP
If
\fBtags\fR
is given for
\fIFILE\fR, tags are imported from other tags\&. Instead of
\fIFORMAT\-NAME\fR
parameters
\fISOURCE\fR
and
\fIEXTRACTION\fR
are required, see
Import from Tags\&. To apply the import from tags on the selected files, use
\fBtagsel\fR
instead of
\fBtags\fR\&. This function also supports output of the extracted value by using an
\fIEXTRACTION\fR
with the value
\fB%{__return}(\&.+)\fR\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBAutomatic import\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBautoimport\fR\ 'u

\fBautoimport\fR
 [\fIPROFILE\-NAME\fR]
 [\fITAG\-NUMBERS\fR]

.PP
Batch import using profile
\fIPROFILE\-NAME\fR
(see
Automatic Import,
\fB"All"\fR
is used if omitted)\&.
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBDownload album cover artwork\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBalbumart\fR\ 'u

\fBalbumart\fR
 {\fIURL\fR}
 [all]

.PP
Set the album artwork by downloading a picture from
\fIURL\fR\&. The rules defined in the
Browse Cover Art
dialog are used to transform general
URLs (e\&.g\&.
from Amazon) to a picture
URL\&. To set the album cover from a local picture file, use the
set
command\&.

.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
kid3\-cli> \fBalbumart
http://www\&.amazon\&.com/Versus\-World\-Amon\-Amarth/dp/B000078DOC\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
.RE
.sp
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBExport to file\fR
.RS 4

.HP \w'\fBexport\fR\ 'u

\fBexport\fR
 {\fIFILE\fR}
